<h1>About MedSpaStack</h1>

<p class="lede">
  MedSpaStack is a pricing and capability research project covering med spa and aesthetics practice
  management software. It is run by one person, and it does one thing carefully rather than many
  things approximately.
</p>

<h2>Who runs it</h2>
<p>
  <strong>Seounghoon Kim</strong> — founder and editor. My background is in software, not in
  aesthetics medicine, and I want that stated plainly at the top rather than buried.
</p>
<p>
  What I bring to this category is not clinical experience. It is a willingness to read
  twenty-four vendors' pricing pages properly, notice that four of them bill per location while
  one bills per user, work out what that does to a real invoice, and publish the result with a date
  on it. That work is unglamorous, nobody else in this niche was doing it consistently, and it
  turns out to be what buyers are missing.
</p>

<h2>What we claim, and what we don't</h2>
<p>
  <strong>We claim accuracy about software.</strong> Prices, tier structures, billing bases,
  onboarding fees, add-on modules, and whether a platform documents genuine clinical charting.
  Every one of those is verifiable from public sources, and we show our source and date on each
  vendor page.
</p>
<p>
  <strong>We do not claim clinical or regulatory authority.</strong> We will not tell you what your
  practice must document, whether a treatment requires supervision, or how your state regulates
  medical direction. Those questions belong to your medical director, your attorney, and your state
  board. A software comparison site answering them would be doing you harm.
</p>
<p>
  Where a question sits on the boundary — for example, whether a platform's consent capture is
  adequate for your treatment mix — we describe the capability and tell you to verify it against
  your own requirements. That is the honest edge of what we can know.
</p>

<h2>Why this exists</h2>
<p>
  Med spa software is sold with headline prices that bear little relationship to the invoice. A
  $15 tier that is actually per user. A $140 tier that is promotional, annual-billing, and per
  location. Charting, e-prescribing, forms and marketing broken out as paid modules. Onboarding
  fees that appear at contract, not on the pricing page.
</p>
<p>
  Meanwhile the comparison content that ranks for these searches is largely written by vendors
  about themselves, or by sites with undisclosed commercial ties to a platform they recommend.
  There was room for someone to just publish the numbers, dated, with the fees included.
</p>

<h2>Contact and corrections</h2>
<p>
  Corrections, vendor pricing changes, and questions: <strong>hello@medspastack.net</strong>
</p>
<p>
  If you are a vendor and something here is factually wrong, send the source and it will be fixed
  and re-dated quickly. If you disagree with an editorial classification rather than a fact, send
  your position and we will publish it alongside ours. We do not take pages down because a vendor
  dislikes an accurate finding.
</p>
<p>
  If you are a buyer who was quoted something different from what we publish, that is the most
  useful email we can receive. Real quotes are how we find out where published pricing has drifted.
</p>

